<h2>A First Fluxfile</h2>
<p>
  A Fluxfile is a version-controlled list of encode jobs, written in YAML (<code>Fluxfile.yaml</code>) or JSON (<code>Fluxfile.json</code>), that <code>flux apply</code> validates as a whole before running in dependency order.
</p>

{{ terminalFrame({
  title: "Fluxfile.yaml",
  stream: "YAML Manifest",
  code: "# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://gitlab.com/sgkens/fluxencoder/-/raw/main/docs/schema/fluxfile-v1.schema.json
version: 1

defaults:
  quality: high
  audio-codec: aac

jobs:
  master:
    input: raw/take1.mov
    output: out/master.mkv
    codec: hevc

  web:
    input: \"@master\"          # consumes the output of job 'master'
    output: out/web.mp4
    codec: h264
    quality: balanced

  trailers:
    input: raw/trailers/      # every media file in the directory
    output: out/trailers/
    preset: web               # named preset from settings.json"
}) }}

<p>Run from the directory containing <code>Fluxfile.yaml</code>:</p>

{{ terminalFrame({
  title: "bash — flux apply lifecycle",
  stream: "stdout",
  exitCode: 0,
  code: "flux apply --check      # validate syntax, schema, and DAG; needs no FFmpeg, touches no file
flux apply --dry-run    # probe and plan, show each FFmpeg command
flux apply              # execute jobs in dependency order
flux apply              # idempotent re-run: finished outputs are skipped"
}) }}

