<h2>Manifest Semantics</h2>

<h3>Values Are Text</h3>
<p>
  YAML 1.1/1.2 implicit typing does not apply. Values such as <code>no</code>, <code>1:30</code>, and <code>010</code> remain the exact text written in the file, and each option parses its own value—so <code>start: 1:30</code> always means 90 seconds and never a sexagesimal integer. Quote values that begin with YAML indicator characters (<code>@</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>!</code>, or <code>{</code>).
</p>

<h3>Variable Substitution (<code>${NAME}</code>)</h3>
<p>
  Variables are expanded in string values in a single non-recursive pass:
</p>

{{ specTable({
  caption: "VARIABLE EXPANSION SYNTAX",
  headers: ["Syntax", "Resolution Behavior"],
  codeCols: [0],
  rows: [
    ["${NAME}", "Replaced with the value of <code>NAME</code>. Refused with exit code <code>2</code> if unset or empty."],
    ["${NAME:-fallback}", "Replaced with the value of <code>NAME</code>, or <code>fallback</code> when unset or empty."],
    ["$$", "Replaced with a literal <code>$</code> character."]
  ]
}) }}

<p>
  Values resolve from <code>--var NAME=VALUE</code> first, then from environment variables prefixed with <code>FLUX_</code>. Arbitrary environment variables (such as <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> or <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code>) are never read.
</p>

{{ terminalFrame({
  title: "bash — passing variables to flux apply",
  stream: "stdout",
  code: "FLUX_MEDIA=/srv/media flux apply --var NAME=episode-01"
}) }}

<h3>Path Resolution & Containment</h3>
<p>
  Relative paths resolve against the directory containing the Fluxfile, never the current working directory. Output paths must resolve inside the Fluxfile's directory unless <code>flux apply --allow-outside</code> is explicitly supplied.
</p>

<h3>JSON Format (<code>Fluxfile.json</code>)</h3>
<p>
  <code>Fluxfile.json</code> uses the identical structure. Comments (<code>//</code>) and trailing commas are permitted:
</p>

{{ terminalFrame({
  title: "Fluxfile.json",
  stream: "JSON Manifest",
  code: "{
  \"$schema\": \"https://gitlab.com/sgkens/fluxencoder/-/raw/main/docs/schema/fluxfile-v1.schema.json\",
  \"version\": 1,
  \"jobs\": {
    \"web\": { \"input\": \"raw/take1.mov\", \"output\": \"out/web.mp4\", \"quality\": \"balanced\" }
  }
}"
}) }}

<h2>Execution Flow & Failure Isolation</h2>

{{ signalPipeline({
  title: "FLUX APPLY VALIDATION & DAG EXECUTION FLOW",
  stages: [
    {
      step: "STEP 01",
      owner: "ManifestParser",
      name: "Safe Event Parse",
      detail: "Parse YAML or JSON. Refuse anchors, aliases, custom tags, duplicate keys, files > 1 MB, or nesting > 32 levels with line:column diagnostics (Exit 2).",
      tag: "YamlDotNet EventReader"
    },
    {
      step: "STEP 02",
      owner: "ManifestValidator",
      name: "Validate Graph & Options",
      detail: "Verify keys, ${NAME} variables, path containment, @job references, dependency cycles, and option values through EncodeCommand.",
      tag: "--check exits 0 here"
    },
    {
      step: "STEP 03",
      owner: "Topological Sort",
      name: "Order Job Graph",
      detail: "Schedule dependencies (needs and @job producers) first while preserving file declaration order for independent jobs.",
      tag: "Deterministic DAG"
    },
    {
      step: "STEP 04",
      owner: "ApplyCommand",
      name: "Idempotent Execution",
      detail: "Skip existing outputs unless --overwrite. If a job fails, block only its downstream dependents and continue independent branches unless --fail-fast.",
      tag: "Exit 0 / 10 / 130"
    }
  ]
}) }}

<h2>Untrusted Manifest Restrictions</h2>
<p>
  Because a Fluxfile may be cloned from an external repository, <code>flux apply</code> treats every manifest as untrusted input:
</p>

{{ specTable({
  caption: "REFUSED CONSTRUCTS IN FLUXFILES",
  headers: ["Disallowed Construct", "Security Rationale"],
  codeCols: [0],
  rows: [
    ["ffmpeg-arg", "Raw FFmpeg arguments can read or write arbitrary local files or open network sockets. Use <code>flux encode --ffmpeg-arg</code> for interactive one-off runs."],
    ["ffmpeg, ffprobe", "Allowing a manifest to pick the executable path would permit arbitrary program execution. Pass <code>--ffmpeg</code> on the CLI instead."],
    ["Non-FLUX_* environment variables", "Prevents a malicious manifest from exfiltrating ambient secrets into remote URLs or output filenames."],
    ["Outputs outside Fluxfile directory", "Prevents overwriting arbitrary files on disk unless the operator passes <code>--allow-outside</code>."],
    ["YAML anchors, aliases, and tags", "Prevents billion-laughs memory amplification and arbitrary type instantiation."],
    ["dry-run, json, quiet, verbose, no-color, fail-fast, no-config", "Invocation-level controls belong to the operator running <code>flux apply</code>, not the manifest."]
  ]
}) }}
